rar-rs is a Rust implementation of the RAR archive format for software
conservancy, interoperability, and educational purposes. It provides `rar`
and `unrar` command-line tools. Original project portions are BSD-2-Clause;
third-party portions retain the licenses identified below.

Implementation sources
----------------------

The RAR5 LZSS+Huffman codec
(`crates/rar/src/codec/lzss_huff/`) was independently implemented from the
public RAR format and from analysis of libarchive's
`archive_read_support_format_rar5.c` by Grzegorz Antoniak (2018), an
independent BSD-2-Clause implementation. It is not copied from proprietary
RARLAB source code.

RAR4 support combines original project code with the upstream-derived decoder
portions identified in the `rars` section below. The current implementation
supports RAR 1.5–4.x reading and RAR4 archive creation, including the relevant
legacy codecs, filters, encryption, solid chains, and volume handling. It does
not contain, link to, or derive from the proprietary `unrar` source code
distributed by RARLAB. Archive structures are implemented from public format
descriptions and independent open-source references.

Portions from the `rars` project
--------------------------------

The RAR5 recovery-record codec
(`crates/rar/src/recovery/rar50.rs`), the BLAKE2sp hash
(`crates/rar/src/rar50/blake2sp.rs`) and the RAR5 key-derivation / hash-key
MAC patterns (`crates/rar/src/crypto/rar50.rs`) were ported from the `rars`
project (https://github.com/bitplane/rars, crates.io `rars`) at upstream
revision `c08a17b`. At that revision, the `rars` workspace declared
`MIT OR Apache-2.0` in its Cargo metadata and did not yet contain its later
WTFPL `COPYING` file. These portions therefore retain the upstream
`MIT OR Apache-2.0` terms. Other project contributions by yjdyamv are
licensed under BSD-2-Clause unless otherwise noted.

Further `rars` portions were ported later: the automatic x86 filter scan
(`crates/rar/src/codec/filters.rs`, `auto_x86_filter_ranges`), the binary-tree
match finder (`crates/rar/src/codec/match_finder.rs`, LZMA-style BT4), the
optimal parse and block splitter, the E8/E8E9 x86 transform normalization,
and the RAR 3.x/4.x, RAR 2.x and RAR 1.5 member decoders. The definition-site
notices currently identify `crates/rar/src/codec/rar29_encoder.rs` as
MIT OR Apache-2.0, and identify the decode-side legacy codec ports in
`rar15.rs`, `rar20.rs`, `rar29.rs`, and `ppmd.rs` as WTFPL. The `rars`
workspace metadata declares `MIT OR Apache-2.0`, while a later `COPYING` file
in that repository declares WTFPL with a no-blame clause; this notice does not
treat those distinct statements as interchangeable. Other ported portions
must be checked against their corresponding upstream file and revision.

The legacy fixture directories under `crates/rar/tests/fixtures/rar40/`
(`rar300/`, `rar2/`, `rar154/`, `multivol/`, `rarvm/`) contain archive files
copied from the `rars` test corpus. They are test inputs, not source code; any
redistribution of those binary fixtures remains subject to the terms applicable
to their original corpus.

Official RAR/UNRAR binaries are used strictly as black-box interoperability
references for testing (set `SA_OFFICIAL_RAR` / `SA_OFFICIAL_UNRAR` to run
the `official_*` interop tests). Their code is not included in this project
and is not used as source code.

Scope and Limitations
----------------------

This software:

  - Does NOT supersede, replace, or replicate the full capabilities of the
    native RAR archiver produced by RARLAB (Alexander Roshal). The proprietary
    RAR encoder uses advanced techniques (including optimal parsing, context
    modelling, and PPMd) that this implementation does not attempt to reproduce.
    Compression ratios will vary; this implementation may produce larger output
    than the native tool in many real-world scenarios.

  - Provides RAR5/RAR7 read and write support, plus RAR 1.5–4.x reading and
    RAR4 archive creation. RAR3 and earlier archive creation is not provided.
    Legacy-format support has feature-specific limitations documented by the
    library and CLI.

  - Is intended for software conservancy, interoperability, and educational use.
    It enables reading and writing of the RAR archive format without depending
    on proprietary binaries.

